In the age of the first dawn, there lived two spirits: Ignis, the Giver, and Terra, the Keeper. Ignis possessed five golden embers, the seeds of life. To multiply their warmth, Ignis had to cast these seeds across the abyss into the garden of Terra. If Terra remained honest, the embers would bloom into a garden of fifteen, and they would share the bounty. But if Terra grew greedy, the embers would vanish into the dark soil of her heart, leaving Ignis cold.

They played this dance under the watching stars. Ignis would always hesitate, weighing the risk of isolation against the promise of abundance. Trust was not a pact, but a leap over a chasm. When Ignis cast the seeds, the air vibrated with potential. If Terra returned the harvest, the world flourished. If she withheld, the embers dimmed, and the chasm grew wider. This myth of embers teaches that prosperity is a fragile thread, woven by two souls who must choose to believe in the other despite the whisper of shadows. It is the ancient gamble of existence, where the greatest gain is found only when one hand trusts the other to hold the weight of their combined light.

